      "claim_excerpt": "## Result\n\nChanged hypothesis for `bit-new-register-model`: free per-register **address twists** `B -> P B Q` (`P, Q` in `GL_m(Z_(2))`) between paid calls, together with free binary mixing and any number of restored dirty roles. The three-register bound (#468) and all its successors explicitly exclude these. Twisted XORs put operators in matrices over the skew group ring `F2[M_m(Q)] \u22ca G`, a nonabelian operation family on an abelian address group.\n\n- **Theorem A (written proof, PROOF.md \u00a73).** Suppose every frame has odd denominators and every twist reduces mod 2 to a one-sided map (`X -> P\u0304X` for all twists, or `X -> XQ\u0304` for all twists). Then any exact word needs `\u03a3_s rank_F2(C_s mod 2)` paid calls. For three targets invertible mod 2 that is **nine**, and the direct word attains it. One-sided twists therefore do not escape the obstruction. The proof extends #2110's square-zero map: twists are equivariant for it, and one-sidedness keeps each call rank one in a suitable flattening.\n- **Proposition B (finite exact).** For two-sided twists, take `\u03c3(B) = S B S^T`, a simultaneous swap of axes 0 and 1. An explicit **seven-call** word for `diag([I],[I],[I])` agrees with the target in the full square-zero image `M_3(\u039b \u22ca G)` in all 5040 call orders. Exact skew-ring products show that **none** is a real identity. Hence no square-zero argument (Theorem A, #2110) can prove nine once two-sided twists are free. Whether they actually beat nine is **open**. No seven-call word and no sub-nine bound are claimed.\n\nThis is a scoped proof plus a finite relaxation witness, not a complete theorem",
